1-4. Search for old-fashioned puzzles (e.g. by Sam Loyd) and try to solve them. Think of how you would make the puzzle.

 Great Picnic Puzzle.

 When they started off on the great annual picnic every wagon in town was pressed into service, each one carrying the same number of people.
 Half way to the picnic ground ten wagons broke down, so it was necessary for each of the remaining wagons to carry one more person.
 When they started for home it was discovered that fifteen more wagons were out of commission, so on the return trip there were three persons more in each wagon than when they started out in the morning.
 Now who can tell how many people attended the great annual picnic?

 If the number of wagons is y, and they contain x number of people.
 (y - 10) wagons can carry (x + 1) people each.
 (y - 25) wagons can carry (x + 4) people each.
 If total number of people left is P:
 	P = y * x
 , then after the first distribution, P is same:
 	P = (y - 10) * (x + 1)
 , then after the second distribution:
 	P = (y - 25) * (x + 3)
 We can simlify:
 	xy = xy - 10x + y - 10 = xy - 25x + 3y - 75
	0 = y - 10x - 10 = 3y - 25x - 75 (*3)
	0 = 3y - 30x - 30 = 3y - 25x - 75
	0 = 5x - 45
	x = 9
	y = 10 * 9 + 10 = 100
	P = 9 * 100 = 900

 The answer is 900 people left the town on 100 wagons.

